WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.880
If the window waits for inhibition, what happens in a mouse that cannot release

2
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:08.880
enough GABA?

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.560
Inhibitory neurons make GABA with two enzymes.

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.840
One, called GAD65, sits at the synaptic terminal.

5
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.550
It tops up the supply when the neuron fires hard.

6
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.670
A knockout lacks a working copy of that gene.

7
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:25.910
Pause and predict.

8
00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.130
Inhibition quiets neurons.

9
00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.270
Should a knockout's cortex shift more than normal when one eye closes?

10
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.320
It does not shift at all.

11
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.840
Its neurons still respond to vision.

12
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:44.800
What failed was the change.

13
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:51.463
Now add diazepam. It binds the GABA receptor at a site between an alpha and a gamma

14
00:00:51.463 --> 00:00:52.463
subunit.

15
00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:55.753
It makes the receptor respond more strongly to GABA.

16
00:00:56.573 --> 00:00:58.233
But it cannot open the channel alone.

17
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:02.187
So it amplifies the GABA the knockout still releases.

18
00:01:03.247 --> 00:01:05.547
Given during deprivation, it restored the shift.

19
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.890
The drug did not shift the cortex by itself.

20
00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.710
The shift went toward the eye that stayed open.

21
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:14.123
Inhibition is permissive.

22
00:01:14.943 --> 00:01:17.223
The difference between the eyes is instructive.

23
00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:19.323
Pause and predict.

24
00:01:20.403 --> 00:01:23.683
Raise a knockout to day 60, long past the normal window.

25
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:26.463
Give diazepam and close one eye.

26
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:36.457
It shifts. The capacity waited into adulthood.

27
00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.957
Age had not closed a window that had never opened.

28
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:45.427
Give diazepam to a normal mouse at about day 15, before its window.

29
00:01:46.387 --> 00:01:47.387
It shifts early.

30
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:53.217
Give a second group two days of diazepam after eye opening, then close one eye at

31
00:01:53.217 --> 00:01:54.217
day 28.

32
00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:56.487
Much less shift is left.

33
00:01:57.307 --> 00:02:00.067
The window opened early and was gone by day 28.

34
00:02:01.167 --> 00:02:06.447
In knockouts, two days of diazepam near the start of deprivation switched plasticity

35
00:02:06.447 --> 00:02:07.447
fully on.

36
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.200
So in young cortex, a brief boost of inhibition can start a window without having to

37
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.200
stay present.

38
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.030
This should feel backward.

39
00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:19.510
Why would less inhibition make a cortex harder to change?

40
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:24.770
The inhibition implicated here is thought to come largely from basket cells.

41
00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.970
Their synapses cluster on the cell bodies of pyramidal neurons.

42
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:35.090
One leading idea: maturing basket-cell inhibition sharpens when pyramidal cells

43
00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.090
fire.

44
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:40.023
That could let the circuit tell which eye reliably drives each cell.

45
00:02:40.983 --> 00:02:43.903
This is a hypothesis, not a settled mechanism.

46
00:02:44.863 --> 00:02:49.043
The experiments establish the order: inhibition first, then plasticity.

47
00:02:50.067 --> 00:02:53.007
Takeaway: inhibition opens the window like a trigger.

48
00:02:53.867 --> 00:02:56.707
The open eye still decides which way the cortex shifts.
